WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.059
Read the frame from its reference interfaces, then locate the equipment supports

2
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.460
and any internal assembly.

3
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.600
A canister is an equipment-supporting assembly within a particular implementation; don't assume

4
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.460
the word establishes one universal O R W layout or interface.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.640
The source-derived plate shows where the shared frame geometry ends and the

6
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.000
selected arrangement begins.

7
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.120
Check rail and support locations, connection references, permitted deflection and the space

8
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.000
needed to insert or remove equipment.

9
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.320
Larger packaging doesn't remove tolerance accumulation or alignment requirements.

10
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.440
Keep the complete populated configuration in view.

11
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.100
An internal assembly can fit within the frame while its hoses or

12
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.400
cables obstruct another item's service path.

13
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.440
A revised support can change the relationship between equipment and a fixed

14
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:44.880
connection.

15
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.880
The review therefore needs both the controlling drawings and evidence that the

16
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.460
chosen assembly works within them.

17
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.160
Use exact source geometry when dimensions matter; avoid inferring manufacturing details from

18
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.660
a simplified rack illustration.

